    {
      "id": 31,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read this section",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 34,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 6
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "Local models cost **$0 on the LiteLLM meter**, so a spend panel can never show what they are worth. This section prices their tokens at a **reference cloud price you choose** (variables above): the default is **gpt-5**, because `litellm/config.yaml` wires `fallbacks` and `context_window_fallbacks` from every local route to gpt-5 — each successful local token is literally a gpt-5 token not bought. Switch to *open-weight host median* for the conservative \"what would DeepInfra/OpenRouter charge for the same GGUF\" floor.\n\n**Two token meters.** *Proxy* = LiteLLM counters (`$local_models`), 30d history, but only calls that crossed LiteLLM, with cached-input tokens priced at the cache rate. *llama-server* = counters read from the loaded model itself (`llamaswap_upstream_*`, since 2026-09-11): sees **every** caller including bebop/pi/OpenClaw hitting llama-swap directly, and its prompt count already excludes cache hits. Where the two disagree, the difference is direct traffic the proxy never saw.\n\n**Net** subtracts only the RTX 3090's metered draw × `$kwh_price`. Hardware amortisation and host power are not included. A high *failures → gpt-5 fallback* number means local calls errored and the cost moved to the cloud — that is spend, not saving."
      }
    },
